WEBVTT

s01-1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.400
Before building roads, guess: where will it jam?

s01-2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.533
All we know is where each trip starts and ends.

s02-1
00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:16.294
Spread each trip evenly over the blocks its rectangle covers.

s03-1
00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:22.066
Before routing a chip, we ask the same question.

s03-2
00:00:22.066 --> 00:00:27.310
Pins are the stops; a net is a group to be linked.

s03-3
00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:30.999
No wires yet; where will they crowd?

s05-1
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.922
One net: 7 wide, 4 tall, HPWL = 11.

s05-2
00:00:44.922 --> 00:00:48.056
Its box covers 6 tiles.

s06-1
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.860
Each tile gets 11 ÷ 6.

s07-1
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.600
Six nets, stacked one by one.

s07-2
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.200
The brighter, the more crowded.

s08-1
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.694
Each tile carries 2; the rest is overflow.

s09-1
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.744
Spread out: total overflow 22.

s09-2
00:01:11.744 --> 00:01:14.989
The worst tile is 14/3 over.

s09-3
00:01:14.989 --> 00:01:19.122
Demand adds up to 34: the total wirelength.

s10-1
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.193
Crowd everything into the middle?

s10-2
00:01:23.193 --> 00:01:26.193
Total overflow drops to 5.

s10-3
00:01:26.193 --> 00:01:29.693
But one tile must carry 7.

s11-1
00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:40.512
A smaller total, a sharper hotspot.

s12-1
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.156
A pin sits on a tile edge: which tile?

s12-2
00:01:47.156 --> 00:01:51.656
Round down: x = 3 belongs to the tile on the right.

s12-3
00:01:51.656 --> 00:01:55.344
Write the rule down, and the numbers repeat.

s13-1
00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.570
Tools share demand by overlap area.

s13-2
00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:08.703
Then spread has the worse tile: 5.54 vs 5.

s14-1
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.927
A real chip has millions of nets.

s14-2
00:02:17.927 --> 00:02:22.727
A placer redraws this map at every step.

s14-3
00:02:22.727 --> 00:02:27.749
So it must be cheap: one box per net.

s14-4
00:02:27.749 --> 00:02:31.882
It does not route, and ignores layers and direction.

s14-5
00:02:31.882 --> 00:02:36.016
It is a weather forecast before routing.

s14-6
00:02:36.016 --> 00:02:40.149
After real routing, will the hotspots still be there?

s15-1
00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:55.033
Go to the lab and drag these pins yourself.

s15-2
00:02:55.033 --> 00:03:00.833
Music: We Never (Remix) · Hi Noise / Qiucheng

